.TH ln "" "Command"
.SH NAME
ln \- create a link to a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBln [\-f] \fIoldfile newfile\fR
.br
\fBln [\-f] \fIfile ... directory\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
ln -- create a link to a file
Usage:	ln [ -f ] oldfile newfile
	ln [ -f ] file ... directory
Option:	-f	Force link even if new file exists
.HE
Each file is known by an i-node number, and is
.I linked
to one or more names, each stored in a directory; the same file can
therefore be known by several names, in several directories.
.B ln
creates a new link to a file.
.PP
In its first form,
.B ln
links the name
.I newfile
to the file already named
.IR oldfile ,
provided
.I newfile
does not already exist.
If the last argument is instead an existing directory,
.B ln
links each
.I file
into that directory under its own last-component name.
With exactly two arguments where the second is not a directory, the
name given as
.I newfile
is used directly.
.IP \fB\-f\fR
If
.I newfile
(or the like-named entry that would be created in
.IR directory )
already exists and is not itself a directory,
.B ln
removes it first and then makes the link; without
.BR \-f ,
an existing
.I newfile
is left alone and the link fails.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cp ,
.BR ls ,
.BR mv ,
.B rm
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B ln
prints a system error message and continues with the next file if a
link cannot be made.
.SH NOTES
Links across file systems are impossible: if two file systems are
mounted, say, on
.B /
and
.BR /usr ,
a file on one cannot be linked to a name on the other.
.PP
.B ln
does not prevent linking a directory to another name; the source
predates symbolic links, and this feature should be avoided.
